mcs51 peephole: add the 'retrack' port hook and rule modifier

A peephole replacement invalidates the register-tracking state the code
generator built for the replaced region.  A rule marked 'retrack'
(written between "replace" and '{', like "restart") asks the port to
re-run its register tracker over that region right after the
replacement, so later rules can act on the refreshed state.

This is what makes mcs51 rule 1013 profitable: rewriting 'mov %1,#0x00'
into 'clr a' / 'mov %1,a' pays off only because the following rtrack
pass then knows a holds 0x00 and turns later #0x00 loads into the
shorter register form.

Implementation:
  - Like the existing optional PORT peephole hooks canJoinRegs and
    canSplitReg, the hook is NULL for ports that do not implement it.
    PORT initialization is positional, so every initializer gains the
    slot (ds390 x3, f8 x2, hc08 x2, mos6502 x2, pdk x3, pic14, pic16,
    stm8, z80 x13); only mcs51 provides an implementation.
  - _mcs51_retrack (rtrack.c) walks the lineNode region at *head, feeds
    each line to _mcs51_rtrackUpdate with comment generation suppressed,
    and splices any replacement lines emitted via emitcode back into the
    peephole list.  It stops at labels, unconditional jumps and returns,
    where the tracked state would no longer hold.
  - In SDCCpeeph.c the flag parser gains the 'retrack' keyword, struct
    peepRule gains a retrack:1 bit carried through newPeepRule, and the
    shared applyRule helper calls the hook after replaceRule on both the
    first-match and tail paths.

Part of a series splitting a larger mcs51 peephole set into
independent pieces.

